## Explanation

A `char` array is a collection of `char`  character  variables stored in contiguous memory locations. In C, `char` arrays are fundamental for handling text, often referred to as "strings." When you create a `char` array, you must define its size, which determines how many characters it can hold. Remember that C arrays are zero-indexed, meaning the first element is at index `0`. For strings, C automatically adds a special null terminator character  `\0`  at the end, so you need to ensure your array is large enough to accommodate both your text and this terminator.

## Visual Model

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[char myString[6]] --> B{Memory Layout};
    B --> C0[Index 0: 'H'];
    B --> C1[Index 1: 'e'];
    B --> C2[Index 2: 'l'];
    B --> C3[Index 3: 'l'];
    B --> C4[Index 4: 'o'];
