Short answer: In C-like languages (C, C++, Java, C#, JavaScript), break stops a switch from “falling through” to the next case. Put break at the end of each case you want to end.

Example (Java/JS/C/C# style):
switch (code) {
  case 200:
    message = "OK";
    break;                 // exits the switch
  case 400:
  case 404:                // grouped cases share the same body
    message = "Client error";
    break;
  default:
    message = "Unknown";
    break;                 // often optional at the end, but fine to include
}

Without break, execution continues into the next case (fall-through):
switch (n) {
  case 1:
    // do something
    // no break here → continues into case 2
  case 2:
    // also runs if n was 1
    break;
}

Notes by language:
- C, C++, Java, C#, JavaScript: Use break to prevent fall-through. You can intentionally omit it to fall through (prefer grouping cases as shown for clarity).
- Go: Each case breaks automatically. Use fallthrough to continue to the next case.
- Swift, Kotlin, Rust, Python (match): No fall-through; break isn’t used to exit a case.

Tip: break exits only the innermost switch (or loop). If you’re inside a function, you can also use return to exit immediately instead of break.

